HEAD:.triage/c/findings/FTQ-010.ttl:6:<urn:beads-cache:finding:FTQ-010> triage:qa5Evidence "{\"finding\": {\"evidence\": \"fixture_child.rs:81-113 reads PID registry once, then kills direct child; common/repo.rs:209-228 performs input=$(cat) before managed_hang PID registration; published sc-cron-0.1.1/src/process.rs:387 uses ProcessGroup::leader(). Source-free probe /tmp/phase-c-execution/c-1/qa-5/flaky-pre-registration-probe.py injects a temporary PATH cat delay using the existing built cron_hurry binary. Probe log records test exit 101 at its 5.01s supervisor timeout, with cat PID 39029 and Python stub shell PID 39028, PGID 39028, still alive at 5.5156s after test start. Explicit probe killpg removed both; AFTER_EXPLICIT_PROBE_CLEANUP is [].\", \"file\": \"crates/bd-cache-store/tests/support/fixture_child.rs\", \"id\": \"FTQ-010\", \"issue\": \"The complete fixture deadline kills/reaps its direct test child but can leave a Python process group alive before PID registration. Cleanup snapshots owned-child PID files once, while the Python stub runs input=$(cat) and other commands before writing that registry entry. A stalled command before registration therefore runs in a separate group that is absent from cleanup. Killing the test process removes its runtime and reaper while the Python group survives. The existing stalled/unwind negatives wait until after PID registration and do not cover this path.\", \"line\": 100, \"mechanism\": \"missing_reap\", \"recommendation\": \"Own launched process groups from before they can execute or use a supervisor/subreaper design that covers unregistered launches and prevents spawn during cleanup. Preserve existing strict bounds and actual joins. Add a bounded pre-registration stall negative with process-group disappearance and reap proof; do not rely only on a registry written by the child after startup.\", \"severity\": \"important\", \"test\": \"Python lifecycle fixtures, including rejected_second_start_cannot_reset_the_first_workers_shutdown_deadline\"}, \"probe\": \"/tmp/phase-c-execution/c-1/qa-5/flaky-pre-registration-probe.log\", \"source\": \"QA5 /root/c1_flaky5 exact2111df6f\", \"status\": \"open\"}" .
HEAD:.triage/c/findings/RSH-001.ttl:2:<urn:beads-cache:finding:RSH-001> triage:id "RSH-001" ; triage:sprint "c-1" ; triage:severity "critical" ; triage:location "crates/bd-cache-sync/src/cron.rs:198" ; triage:source "qa-3 owning reviewer fenced JSON" ; triage:status "open" ; triage:record "{\"carry_forward_from\": \"qa-2 specialist output; unchanged production source, still open\", \"evidence\": \"OPEN at assigned HEAD: crates/bd-cache-sync/src/cron.rs:198 still constructs worker.shutdown(now + shutdown_timeout), :202 awaits it, :214 awaits that same future after a hurried stop, and :217-225 calls the ordinary expiry kill/callback only after shutdown returns. Cargo.toml:36 and Cargo.lock:1153-1154 remain published sc-cron exactly 0.1.1. Its actual installed registry source /Users/randlee/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/sc-cron-0.1.1/src/scheduler.rs:912-931 still times out then awaits task without abort; worker.rs:539-550 still awaits an uncancelled blocking store call. Thus the original contended reload/persistence occurrence remains. No workaround or baseline change was introduced. The sprint explicitly records this blocker at docs/plans/phase-c/sprint-c-1-runtime-migration.md:511 and :540-545.\", \"file\": \"crates/bd-cache-sync/src/cron.rs\", \"id\": \"RSH-001\", \"issue\": \"The migrated shutdown call no longer enforces the existing bounded stop contract. Published sc-cron 0.1.1 waits for its worker even after the handle deadline. A worker awaiting a contended shared-store call can therefore keep cron_run_hurried beyond T and its documented 2*T total bound; on the ordinary expiry path the bd-kill callback is itself delayed until that wait ends. A second signal also awaits the same unresolved shutdown future at line 211. This violates REQ-BDC-0730 and the preserved REQ-CRON-0012 semantics.\", \"line\": 198, \"recommendation\": \"Resolve the mismatch upstream and qualify an approved bounded-shutdown contract before accepting the migration. Ensure expiry triggers kill immediately and cleanup finishes within the existing absolute shutdown bound, including a worker blocked in reload/persistence. Do not fork the scheduler or replace the bound with an async timeout that leaves detached ownership behind.\", \"reviewer\": \"rust-service-hardening-agent\", \"severity\": \"critical\", \"topic\": \"graceful_shutdown\"}" .
